{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790342644,"nonce":"vPlzdydcWZ4OVdxyM3/6+enwmi4=","title":"GPU kernel backends and the zyntax kernel module","message":"The direction decided 2026-09-25 (docs/GPU_AOT_ARCHITECTURE.md and docs/ml-dsl-plans/09-gpu-compute-system.md, \"Status and direction (September 2026)\"):\n\n- @kernel is a Zyntax capability every frontend reaches through a `zyntax` module in its own idiom (Python `from zyntax import kernel`, a Lua zyntax table, ZynML natively). Kernels are a typed subset; dynamic code inside is a compile error.\n- HIR with first-class vectors is the kernel IR; there is no separate compute IR.\n- Apple first: MSL generated from HIR, compiled at run time by the Metal framework (no Xcode or Metal toolchain on the user's machine), host side on objc2-metal; GEMM-shaped kernels dispatch to Accelerate (small and medium) or MPS (large).\n- NVIDIA: HIR to LLVM IR to in-process NVPTX (already linked through inkwell llvm21-1) to PTX, loaded through cudarc with dynamic loading. Tile IR bytecode through cutile-ir is a second track after SIMT works.\n- Portable (Vulkan, WebGPU) deferred; naga IR is the emitter when it returns.\n- Benchmarks: PyTorch eager and torch.compile, numba, JAX/XLA, Taichi, numpy, Triton where CUDA exists, then candle; CPU on the Mac and the NUC, GPU on the Mac through Metal against PyTorch MPS and candle Metal; steady state and time to first result.\n\nNeither the Mac nor the NUC has an NVIDIA GPU; NVIDIA execution needs an sm_80+ Linux machine.\n\nSteps, in order: frontend `zyntax` module surfaces for Python and Lua; kernel type checking; Metal backend; benchmark harness; NVPTX backend; Tile IR.","files":null}]}